'''''Hokta''''' (died 1527) was the miko, or chief, of the [[Akwesa]], a chiefdom of the northwestern coast of [[Lurandia]], during the period of the Zong colonial presence at the bay of [[Oktalwa]]. He held no title higher than that of miko, the Akwesa term for the office, and is rendered as chief in the Zong record. After his death the Zong colonial administration conferred on him the title King of the Northern Mahiks, which no Zong source of his lifetime uses and which he did not hold. He is known from the [[Zong]] colonial record as the chief who granted the site of the future [[Tamsui]] to Zong traders in 1511, and as the father of [[Chinowa]], whose marriage to [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]] in 1521 established the alliance between the chiefdom and the merchant house of the [[Sūn-heng Hâng]].

Little is recorded of his life. His birth is not attested, and the date of his death is known only from the year given in the Zong record. No account of the Akwesa themselves survives, and Hokta is known almost entirely from documents written by the people who dealt with him.

== Rule ==

Hokta ruled the Akwesa as its chief from before 1511 until his death. His title throughout his life was that of miko, the Akwesa term for the office of chief. The Zong record renders the title as chief, and no document of his lifetime gives him any higher one.

His territory comprised the bay of Oktalwa and its surrounding country, which lay between the lands of other [[Kawesa]] chiefdoms. Those chiefdoms sought access to the bay, and the Zong record describes Hokta's position as one of continuing pressure from neighbours.

The [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]], the large polity to the south, applied to the Akwesa and to the neighbouring peoples the term [[mahik|''mahik'']], "kingless". The term was a pejorative. It described those peoples by the absence of a ruler of the centralised kind the Hahkina recognised — a king of a state with a capital. It was applied to the peoples of the north and west without regard to how they were governed, and it was the Hahkina's description of their neighbours rather than a term they used of themselves.

== The grant of 1511 ==

In 1511 Hokta granted a plot of land at the bay to a party of Zong traders in exchange for ten pigs. The transaction was recorded by the Zong as a purchase. Akwesa practice did not treat land as property that could be alienated, and the grant was one of use, the ten pigs being the customary gift accompanying a grant of hospitality.

The Zong record of the transaction as a sale was not known to the Akwesa, who continued to regard the site as their own and its use as granted.

A depot was built on the plot by the merchant house of [[Chiu Bûn-hái]], trading as the Sūn-heng Hâng, and was operated as a manned outpost from 1511.

== The alliance and the marriage ==

The arrangement between Hokta and the Sūn-heng Hâng developed from a trading relationship into a military one. The company assisted the Akwesa in their wars against neighbouring chiefdoms, and the territory under Akwesa control expanded in consequence.

In 1521 the alliance was sealed by a marriage. Hokta's daughter Chinowa was married to Chiu Khéng-gio̍k, son of Chiu Bûn-hái and then a teenager. The Zong colonial record contains no account of the marriage as a romance between the couple, and it is described as an arrangement concluded between Hokta and the elder Chiu.

The territory that resulted from the alliance was known to the Zong as [[Okheng]] (沃興), a name formed from the first element of Oktalwa and the second of Sūn-heng. It was governed jointly by the two families and functioned as a polity.

== Death ==

Hokta died in 1527, aged about fifty-four on the age given in the Zong record. A birth around 1473 is inferred from that figure, though no year of birth is attested.

He died of plague. The outbreak is the earliest recorded at the bay, and the disease is understood to have been carried there by rats aboard the trading vessels that called at the settlement.

The Akwesa suffered severely. Plague recurred at the settlement in the years that followed, and the losses among the chiefdom are described in the colonial papers as heavy. The record does not give numbers, and no account of the epidemics was kept by the Akwesa themselves. The chiefdom was much reduced by the deaths, and its capacity to maintain itself against the pressure of neighbouring chiefdoms declined in the same period.

The epidemic at Tamsui is distinct from the [[Jade Sea Plague]] of the early 15th century, which was confined to [[Fosia]].

He had no sons and two daughters, [[Chinowa]] and [[Oktisa]]. Succession among the Akwesa followed male descent, and the absence of a son left the chiefdom without a direct heir. This is the context in which the marriage of 1521 was concluded: the union of Chinowa with [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]] supplied the chiefdom with a male figure of standing connected to Hokta's line, at the same time as it secured the alliance with the Sūn-heng Hâng.

He was succeeded as miko by Chiu Khéng-gio̍k, the office passing to the husband of his daughter rather than to a son of his own line. Chiu Khéng-gio̍k held the title for the remainder of his life, and retained it after the Akwesa ceased to be governed as a chiefdom, so that the office continued after the political entity to which it belonged had ended.

The territory passed at the same time under his rule as the polity of [[Okheng]], the joint holding of the two families. On Hokta's death in 1527 the two offices were therefore held by the same person: the rule of the merged territory, and the chieftainship of the people from whom it had been formed. The succession was determined in the midst of the outbreak that had killed Hokta, and among a people that had lost many of its number to it.

== King of the Northern Mahiks ==

The title by which Hokta is chiefly known in later Zong and modern accounts was conferred after his death and was not his.

By the 1530s the colonial administration had resolved to acquire the coast north of the Hahkina frontier. The region was occupied by six chiefdoms besides the Akwesa, none of which recognised any authority over the others and none of which had granted the colony anything. The administration's difficulty was one of standing: it required a ground on which the lands of the northern chiefdoms could be claimed as the colony's.

The ground adopted was a title. Hokta was declared, posthumously, to have been King of the Northern Mahiks — the ruler not of the Akwesa alone but of the whole region.

The title was devised in the course of the negotiations for the [[Treaty of Anping]] in 1538. Its immediate purpose was to settle the boundary between the territory the Zong were to hold and the territory to be left to the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]]. The two allies required a line, and the line followed the extent of what the Zong could claim to have acquired. If Hokta had been only the miko of the Akwesa, the colony could claim the land of the Akwesa alone; by establishing that he had been king of the whole northern region, the colony could claim the whole of it, and treat the remainder of the coast as its own territory shared out to the Hahkina rather than as land the Hahkina had won.

The claim proceeded in three steps:

* the northern chiefdoms had all been subject to Hokta as their king;
* Hokta had granted his land to the [[Sūn-heng Hâng]], and the company had in 1535 conveyed it to the colony;
* the lands of the northern chiefdoms were therefore the colony's already, and the chiefdoms that did not submit to the colony were not foreign powers but subjects in rebellion.

The title is not attested in any document of Hokta's lifetime. No Zong record before the 1530s describes him as anything other than the miko of the Akwesa, and no chiefdom of the north is recorded as acknowledging his authority.

The [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]] raised no objection to the claim, their interest being in the territory the arrangement assigned to them rather than in the reasoning by which it was divided. Nothing in the Hahkina record indicates that the title was made known to them. It is likely that Hokta, a chiefdom ruler at the far end of the coast who had died eleven years before the treaty, was unknown to them, and that the claim was never put to them in terms that would have required an assessment of it. The claim was devised by [[Tân Bûn-khéng]] (陳文慶), the colonial administration's negotiator at the treaty, and it is the legal foundation on which the [[First Mahik War]] was prosecuted and the region partitioned in 1542. It determined the boundary: the lands attributed to Hokta as king were the lands the Zong took, and the Hahkina took what lay beyond them.

No objection to the claim is recorded from any quarter. The administration advanced it as an instrument rather than as a finding, and the record of the negotiations gives no indication that the question of Hokta's actual standing was treated as one requiring examination. It is consistent with the rest of the colonial papers of the period that the accuracy of the title was not a matter of concern to those who devised it, the object being the acquisition of the coast and the settlement of a boundary with an ally.

His death preceded by eight years the sale of the site to the colonial administration. The alliance he had concluded continued in force, and the territory passed from the joint holding of the two families to the Zong colony in 1535.

== The record ==

Hokta is known from a small number of entries in the colonial papers of [[Anping]]. The Zong record notes his grant of the site, his age at death, and the marriage of his daughter; it contains no account of his reign in his own terms, no genealogy, and no statement of Akwesa practice that is not given in the course of describing an agreement with the colony.

The Akwesa are the only one of the Kawesa chiefdoms whose name survives in the colonial record, and the modern collective name for the peoples of the region descends from it. That circumstance is attributable to the chiefdom's commercial relationship with the colony. Hokta's grant of 1511 is the transaction from which that relationship derived, and the survival of the chiefdom's name in the record follows from it.

